  "text_md": "- Reading: Kim\n\n- Assignment: Finalize paper topic, begin outline\n\n## October 23: International structure and levels of analysis\n\n- Reading: Waltz\n\n## October 24: International structure and levels of analysis (continued)\n\n- Reading: Waltz\n- Assignment: Brief paper outline\n\n## October 25: The intersection between domestic and international relations\n\n- Reading: Gourevitch\n\n- Reading: Putnam\n\n- Reading: Chung\n\n## October 26: Conclusion of the course\n\n- Assignment: Detailed paper outline and presentation\n\n## October 27: In-Class Final Exam\n\n## November 10: Final paper due on Canvas at midnight CST\n\n## Assignments, Grading and Due Dates:\n\n## Class Participation:\n\nStudents must read the assigned material before engaging in discussion and must contribute as much as possible to the in-class discussions. Each student is likely to find considerable benefit in the exchange of ideas, doubts; and criticisms during discussions. Much of the value of a graduate class is in the sharing of the knowledge and perspective that each participant brings to the sessions.\n\n## Final Exam:\n\nA final exam will be distributed the final class session. The exam will cover all readings assigned for the class, as well as material discussed in class. during\n\n## Analytical Paper:\n\nStudents will prepare an analytical paper about an international topic of their choice: The paper will model an outstanding form of analytical writing as discussed in class. It will be 5-7 pages (double-spaced) and will examine and evaluate a contemporary international issue from a particular theoretical perspective and level of analysis. The final paper will be due on November 10 via Canvas.\n\n## Grading:\n\nThis is a letter-graded course: A, B, C, Ds or F. The standard scale will apply: 90%-100% = A; 80%-89% = B; 70%-79% = C; 60%-69% = D; below 60% = F. grade\n\n| Assignment          | Percent of Grade   |\n|---------------------|--------------------|\n| Class Participation | 20%                |\n| Analytical Paper    | 40%                |\n| Final Exam          | 40%                |\n\nNotice: Failure to meet assignment due dates could result in a of I (Incomplete) and may adversely impact Tuition Assistance and/or Financial Aid. grade",
